</section>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A0.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Vision Language Model Calibration</h3>

<div id="A0.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A0.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We describe the implementation procedures for detecting propositions generated by <math id="A0.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\texttt{Q2TL}}\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">Q2TL</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\texttt{Q2TL}}\ </annotation></semantics></math> and using them to annotate individual frames within synthetic video sequences. The calibration process involves two key components: (1) the inference methodology employed by Vision Language Models (VLMs), and (2) the procedure used to determine optimal calibration thresholds that control the false positive rate for each VLM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A0.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Vision Language Model Inference Framework</h3>

<div id="A0.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A0.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Vision Language Models serve as semantic detection mechanisms. Each atomic proposition <math id="A0.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}\in\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}\in\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, including examples such as “person”, “car”, “person in the car”, is processed alongside. Upon receiving binary responses (‘Yes’ or ‘No’) from the VLM, we extract token probabilities from these outputs and utilize them as confidence metrics for detection accuracy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A0.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Determination of False Positive Rate Thresholds</h3>

<section id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Calibration Dataset Construction:</h4>

<div id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The COCO Captions dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. 2015</a>)</cite> serves as the foundation for calibrating several open-source vision language models, including the InternVL2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenGVLab 2024</a>)</cite> and Qwen Models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. 2025</a>)</cite>. Since the dataset contains validated image-caption pairs representing positive associations, we generate negative examples by randomly matching images with captions from different image entries within the dataset. The resulting calibration dataset encompasses 40,000 image-caption pairs, each evaluated by the VLM to produce binary ‘Yes’ or ‘No’ classifications.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Optimal Threshold Selection Framework</h4>

<div id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The identification of optimal VLM thresholds can be approached as either single-class or multi-class classification problems; we implement the multi-class approach. Our methodology begins by aggregating detection results into confidence score arrays paired with one-hot encoded ground truth labels. We systematically evaluate all available confidence scores as potential thresholds, computing prediction accuracy for each candidate threshold. The optimal threshold maximizes overall accuracy, defined as the ratio of combined true positive and true negative predictions to total predictions.</p>
</div>
<div id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A0.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For comprehensive model evaluation, we generate Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curves by calculating true positive rates (TPR) and false positive rates (FPR) across the complete threshold range. Following optimal threshold identification, we apply these calibrated thresholds to VLM predictions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A0.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Evaluations With Larger Frame Input</h3>

<div id="A0.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A0.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct a final evaluation to assess the impact of increasing the number of frames passed to the VLM during answer generation. While <span id="A0.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">NeuS-QA</span> was previously evaluated with 32 input frames (<a href="#S4.T1" title='In ℒ_"Q2TL": Question to Temporal Logic. ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ NeuS-QA: Grounding Long-Form Video Understanding in Temporal Logic and Neuro-Symbolic Reasoning' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), we now increase this to 48. As shown in <a href="#A0.T6" title="In A.5 Evaluation on All LongVideoBench Categories ‣ NeuS-QA: Grounding Long-Form Video Understanding in Temporal Logic and Neuro-Symbolic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, using 48 frames consistently improves accuracy across all VLM backbones, further outperforming both the original 32-frame setup and all baseline models. Empirically, 48 frames provides the best performance in our <span id="A0.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">NeuS-QA</span> pipeline.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A0.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.5 </span>Evaluation on All LongVideoBench Categories</h3>

<div id="A0.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A0.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct a comprehensive evaluation of <span id="A0.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">NeuS-QA</span> across all 17 LongVideoBench categories, which span a broad range of reasoning skills, including spatial grounding, temporal ordering, object-event interactions, and compositional logic. While our core experiments focused on temporally grounded tasks, this broader analysis tests the generality of <span id="A0.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">NeuS-QA</span> across diverse question structures beyond temporal logic.</p>
</div>
<div id="A0.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A0.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#A0.T7" title="In A.5 Evaluation on All LongVideoBench Categories ‣ NeuS-QA: Grounding Long-Form Video Understanding in Temporal Logic and Neuro-Symbolic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, <span id="A0.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">NeuS-QA</span> consistently outperforms its respective base vision-language model across nearly all categories for both LLaVA-OneVision and Qwen2.5-VL. The strongest gains appear in temporally complex categories such as T3E, E3E, and T2A, though improvements are also seen in spatial and compositional tasks. These results underscore the versatility of symbolic segment selection, demonstrating its value as a general-purpose reasoning strategy across long-form video question answering.</p>
</div>
